Anchor the Claude Code fingerprint on its identity phrase, not the bare brand.

llmtrim identifies the calling agent by matching registered markers against the
system prompt (crates/llmtrim-core/src/attribution.rs). The claude-code entry
used the loose substring "Claude Code" and is checked before opencode. opencode
injects its loaded skill descriptions into its own system prompt, and several of
them mention "Claude Code" verbatim (e.g. "creating a Claude Code slash
command", "generating a CLAUDE.md"). Every such opencode session therefore
matched the claude-code marker first and was mislabeled `claude-code` in
`llmtrim status`.

Claude Code's own identity line is "You are Claude Code, Anthropic's CLI..."
(see the crate's attribution tests). Anchor the marker on that phrase — the
same "You are <Agent>," shape every other agent uses — so a prose mention of
the product name no longer wins over opencode's own "You are OpenCode," marker.

Verified: all attribution unit tests pass, real Claude Code still fingerprints
as claude-code, and an opencode prompt containing "Claude Code" prose now
fingerprints as opencode.
